title: Editing the grammar
tags: [[Simplified query language]]
kind: guide
summary: Change the simplified language by editing core/query-grammar — add a predicate, an alias, a shortcut — and reload it; what the load checks.

The grammar is one file, global to all your repositories:
`~/.config/metafolder/core/query-grammar`. The GUI and the CLI read it; the daemon
never does. It lives in the configuration repository, so your edits are merged with
future shipped changes ([[Updating the configuration]]).

Most edits are one line in `predicate` or `field`:

```
# a shortcut: `todo` for the records tagged todo that are not done
  | "todo"                  => tag -> (path = "todo") AND NOT done = true
# an alias: `artist` for the embedded-metadata field
field  = "p"      => mfr_path
       | "artist" => mfr_meta_artist
       | w:WORD   => $w
# a new operator: `f ^ v` for "starts with"
  | f:field "^" w:WORD      => $f MATCHES "^$w"
```

Put a new alternative before any other that would match the same start: choice is
ordered, and the first alternative that succeeds wins ([[Simplified grammar notation]]).
A synonym for an operator goes inside the repetition that uses it —
`("OR" | "+")` — not as a second alternative, which ordered choice would never reach.

The GUI reads the grammar at startup; after an edit, reload it without a restart with
`config:reload grammar` ([[Reloading the configuration]]). The CLI reads it at each `-s`.

!! What the load checks

A grammar is validated when it is loaded, and refused whole with an error naming the
production at fault:

* the start production `query` exists, and every production a rule refers to;
* no ''left recursion'' (`or = or …` would loop forever);
* every string literal lexes to ''exactly one'' token — a literal matches one input token by its text, so `"/fav"` (two tokens) could never match; the error suggests the split form, `"/" "fav"`.

A missing or malformed file is an error when a client loads it — there is no fallback
to a built-in grammar ([[No runtime fallback]]); an empty file is a grammar with no
rules, accepting nothing. The DSL needs no grammar and is unaffected either way.
Expansion errors carry the position in the simplified input.
